# THE GUARD REVISION THIS REPOSITORY AUDITED, BY CONTENT.
#
# .github/workflows/release.yml pins the shared release guards by commit
# SHA. A SHA is content-addressed and therefore immutable, so the pin
# cannot be edited under us. What a SHA does NOT do is tell a reader what
# is in it, and nothing states which BYTES were looked at before pinning
# them. That is what this file is for.
#
# Every file the guards execute is recorded below by SHA-256, taken at
# the pinned revision. tests/test_guard_pins.sh re-fetches each one and
# refuses any difference, so:
#
#   * bumping the pin without re-auditing goes RED, because the digests
#     below describe a revision the workflow no longer names;
#   * a pin that resolves to bytes other than the audited ones goes RED,
#     which is the falsifiable form of "we ran what we pinned";
#   * the check runs offline of any claim the guards make about
#     themselves, before a tag is ever pushed.
#
# WHAT REMAINS TRUSTED, stated plainly rather than papered over: that
# GitHub runs the workflow file living at the pinned SHA. A workflow
# cannot establish its own identity from inside itself, because a
# substituted file could simply print the expected answer. That is the
# platform's guarantee and the reason `uses:@<sha>` is written by SHA.
# Everything downstream of it is checked here by content.
#
# THE DIGESTS BELOW WERE WRITTEN BY `bash fleet/guard_pins.sh`, which
# fetched each file at 2db00705e05b1774cf41eb58006e6533cfca4d55 and hashed it. Do not
# hand-edit them, and do not copy this file from another repository: a
# copied record passes every check while having audited nothing, because
# the digests are genuinely the pinned revision's bytes. What a copy
# cannot carry is the reading.
#
# TO RE-AUDIT AFTER A PIN BUMP: read the diff, then re-run
# `bash fleet/guard_pins.sh <this repository>` and rewrite the note below.
#
#   https://github.com/nelsonduarte/capa-language/compare/<old>...2db00705e05b1774cf41eb58006e6533cfca4d55

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# AUDIT NOTE.
#
# FIRST PIN FOR THIS REPOSITORY, so there is no diff to read. All five
# files below were fetched at 2db0070 and read in full: 608 lines of
# release-guards.yml, 80 of capa_floor.sh, 112 of check_tag_version.sh,
# 223 of clean_room_build.sh and 148 of verify_guard_digests.sh. Their
# digests were also recomputed locally from the fetched bytes and match
# the five lines below.
#
# THIS IS THE FIRST ADOPTEE WITH REAL RUNTIME DEPENDENCIES, so one
# paragraph of release-guards.yml that was hypothetical everywhere else
# is live here. The `Guard 2` step sets `GH_TOKEN: ${{ github.token }}`
# and its comment states why: without a token, `capa install` inside the
# clean room "degrades to the GPG layer and emits 'SLSA provenance not
# verified' warnings while the room still goes green". In the pure
# siblings that covered one dev-dependency. Here it covers capa_base64
# and capa_hash, which supply this package's base64url codec and its
# HMAC, so the token is what makes "the guard verified the provenance of
# every dependency" a true sentence rather than a green log. Nothing in
# this repository can set that input; it is the shared workflow's, and it
# is set at this revision.
#
# TWO THINGS THE PIN SAYS THAT THE COMPILER HAS SINCE MADE UNTRUE, and
# neither reaches this repository:
#
#   * capa_floor.sh's header states that nothing in the compiler enforces
#     the manifest floor and that "this guard is the only thing that
#     tests the claim". capa 1.19.0 changed that: capa/pkg/_floor.py now
#     makes the ROOT floor a hard error and a DEPENDENCY's floor a
#     warning. The script's behaviour is unaffected; only its comment is
#     stale.
#   * its floor grammar is still `[0-9][0-9.]*[0-9]`, which accepts
#     two- and four-component versions that the compiler-side parser now
#     refuses. `>=1.18.1` is read identically as 1.18.1 by both.
#
#   Run against this repository's new manifest at this revision:
#   capa_floor.sh prints 1.18.1, and check_tag_version.sh accepts v0.2.0
#   and refuses v0.1.0 with "manifest says 0.2.0". That is what confirms
#   the long comment blocks now sitting inside [package] parse as neither
#   a second version nor a second floor: both scripts are table-scoped
#   and skip `#` lines, and this repository's comments include lines
#   quoting `capa = '>=99.0.0'` and `max=['Stdio']` from measured output.
#
# THE DEPENDENCY FLOORS ARE NOT THE GUARD'S BUSINESS AT THIS PIN, which
# is worth recording because it looks like it should be. capa_floor.sh
# reads the ROOT manifest only, so the `>=1.1.0` that capa_base64 v0.1.0
# and capa_hash v0.1.2 still declare never reaches the choice of clean-
# room compiler. The clean room downloads 1.18.1, this package's own
# floor, and 1.18.1 predates the dependency-floor warning entirely.
#
# NOTHING IS EXPORT-IGNORED HERE. clean_room_build.sh exists partly
# because capa_authgate shipped a tarball whose README named a script
# `.gitattributes` had removed. This repository's .gitattributes carries
# only `* text eol=lf` and there is no tools/ directory, so the tarball
# is the repository. Confirmed against `git archive`: 25 entries, both
# modules, tests/, capa.toml, capa.lock, publisher.asc and all three
# workflows.
#
# Moving the pin is a fleet decision and is not made here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

